  "step_2": "The individual considers the difference in probabilities between the two lotteries: 33% vs. 34%, which they describe as only a 1% difference. They state that this small difference makes the probabilities 'almost the same' in their view. Based on this, they decide to choose the option with more money—Lottery A (2500 Euros) over Lottery B (2400 Euros). The individual's deliberation focuses on the magnitude of the best outcome (2500 vs. 2400) and the near-equivalence of the probabilities. Their justification is based on selecting the higher monetary outcome when the probabilities are perceived as nearly identical.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires comparing the maximum outcome weighted by its probability. The individual does consider the maximum outcome (2500 vs. 2400) and the probabilities (33% vs. 34%), and they explicitly state that the probability difference is negligible. Their justification aligns with the logic of the reason: they prioritize the higher maximum outcome when probabilities are nearly equal. Although the individual does not explicitly compute the weighted values (825 vs. 816), their reasoning process—favoring the higher outcome when probabilities are perceived as equivalent—mirrors the underlying logic of the decision reason. Therefore, the decision reason was likely used, even if implicitly. The assessment is not certain due to the lack of explicit calculation, but the alignment is strong.",
  "final_assessment_score": 85
